  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 234 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nSatellite/WV imagery shows a deep upper trough crossing the western \nCONUS, centered across the Great Basin, with very moist \nsouthwesterly flow increasing across the northern High Plains. \nSurface obs show low pressure strengthening across eastern WY into \nthe CO Front Range. Southerly winds extending from TX to the Dakotas \nare pushing in plenty of moisture in the low levels. Still looks \nlike the entire CWA will see precip from early this evening to early \nTue morning as this large system crosses the north central CONUS. \nThe three aspects of this storm we are resolving are QPF amounts, \nsnow accumulation, and wind gusts. There is very good model \nagreement with widespread totals of 0.50\" or more across the entire \nCWA. Models over the last 24 hours have settled on central SD \nreceiving the most QPF, which could reach over an inch and a half or \neven 2 inches. Also, the northern Black Hills could see upslope-\nenhanced QPF exceeding two inches (some models suggest near 3 \ninches). Most other locations have a good chance of over an inch. \nThis is an impressive system for early November bringing in PWATs \nover an inch...and approaching 1-1.5 inches over the eastern half of \nSD, which would be considered decently moist for the summer months. \nGiven how dry early November is, that is 2-3 times higher than \naverage. Upcoming soundings will show how well that verifies. Either \nway, the moisture is there for these high QPFs. Colder air will move \nin on the backside of the low during the day tomorrow. While the \nsystem will remain too warm for widespread accumulating snow, the \nhigher elevations of the northern Black Hills should see minor \naccumulations (~2\") after rain switches to snow Monday evening. \nTemps will be marginally cold enough in the evening for snow across \nthe NE WY plains, but overall accumulation will be greatly \nlimited by precip ending before sufficiently cold air arrives. \nHowever, minor accumulations along higher ridges can't be ruled \nout. Winds will increase behind the system as well, as the low \ncloses up Mon afternoon with gusts of 45-55 mph or more expected \nacross the NE WY plains and the western SD plains. The question \nremains just how strong the gusts will end up being. CAMs have \nbeen showing well above 50kt wind gusts across western SD. \nInterestingly, the 18z HRRR has dropped max gusts to 45-55mph, \nwhich is what prior shifts have been settling on. The exception is\n+50kt winds just east of the Black Hills into Fall River County. \nThis lines up nicely with ensemble guidance. A High Wind Watch has\nbeen issued for most of the western SD plains given the potential\nfor warning-level gusts, although portions of the watch area may \nultimately warrant a Wind Advisory instead. Precip ends Tuesday \nmorning, with drier and cooler (but near average) temps through \nmid week. Winds will be gusty early Tuesday but taper off through \nthe day. Quiet weather is expected through the second half of the \nweek.",
